The lifecycle node framework is used extensively through out this project and all servers utilize it. It is best convention for all ROS systems to use lifecycle nodes if it is possible. [...] When a node is started, it is in the unconfigured state, only processing the node's constructor which should not contain any ROS networking setup or parameter reading. By the launch system, or the supplied lifecycle manager, the nodes need to be transitioned to inactive by configuring. After, it is possible to activate the node by transitioning through the activating stage. [...] Skip to content

